.TH AX25RAW 1
.SH NAME
ax25raw \- an AX.25 peer built by hand
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ax25raw hear
.RI [ n ]
.br
.B ax25raw poke
.I dest src text
.RB [ via
.IR path ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ax25raw
assembles and decodes AX.25 frames directly on
.BR /dev/radio0/data ,
from the specification rather than from
.BR /os/inferno/radio/devax25.c :
six characters of callsign shifted left one bit and padded with spaces,
then an SSID byte of command-or-repeated, two reserved bits sent as
ones, four bits of SSID and the end-of-address bit on the last address
only; then control
.B 0x03
for UI and PID
.B 0xf0
for no layer 3.
.PP
It exists because
.IR ax25chk (1)
checks
.IR ax25 (3)
against itself, which cannot catch an address field this tree agrees
with and no TNC does.  It is not somebody else's program, which is its
weakness; it is somebody else's format, written on the other side of a
device boundary with none of
.IR ax25 (3)'s
code reachable from it.
.PP
.B Poke
sends one frame exactly as given, with no checking, because the point
is to be able to send what a correct implementation would not.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
	; ax25raw poke KA9Q-1 N0CALL-7 hand-built
	RAWSENT N0CALL-7>KA9Q-1 26 bytes
	; ax25raw hear 1
	RAW KA9Q-1>W1AW-2 via WIDE1-1,WIDE2-1 UI pid 0xf0 info hopped-0
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/ax25raw.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.I ax25chk (1),
.I ax25 (3),
.I loraraw (1)
.SH BUGS
It speaks only to
.B /dev/radio0/data
and there is no way to point it at another packet device.
.PP
It decodes a control field it does not recognise by printing the byte,
which is the most it can say about a frame whose format it does not
implement.
